<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Farhud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Farhud was a violent pogrom against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> on 1–2 June 1941, in the power vacuum after the fall of Rashid Ali's pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> government and before British troops entered the city. At least 180 Jews were killed, many more were injured and their property was looted. It marked a turning point for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s ancient Jewish community, most of whom left for Israel in 1950–1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/farhud.jpg" alt="Farhud" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Farhud_mass_grave.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the 1930s the position of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s Jews worsened. Arab nationalism had earlier counted them as fellow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, but this changed with the conflict in Mandatory Palestine and Nazi propaganda aimed at the Arab world. In September 1934 tens of Jews were dismissed from the ministry of economics and communications, and unofficial quotas limited their entry to the civil service and to schools and colleges. Between 1932 and 1941 the German embassy under Fritz Grobba supported antisemitic and fascist movements. By 1941 about 150,000 Jews lived in Iraq, and they were widely viewed as pro-British.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 1 April 1941 a group of pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> officers known as the Golden Square, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-ali-al-gaylani">Rashid Ali al-Gaylani</a>, overthrew the regent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-ilah">Abd al-Ilah</a>, in a coup with significant popular support. Fighting with British forces followed, and on 30 May the British column Kingcol reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. In the days before the violence, Jewish houses were reportedly marked with red palm prints, and false rumours spread that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> had signalled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> by radio.</p><p>The pogrom took place on 1 and 2 June 1941, during the Jewish festival of Shavuot. Iraqi government and British sources say it began when a Jewish delegation going to meet the regent was attacked on Al Khurr Bridge, while the scholar Zvi Yehuda argues that anti-Jewish preaching at a mosque set it off. Violence spread through the Al Rusafa and Abu Sifyan districts, shops were burned, a synagogue was destroyed and police joined in on the second day. Some Muslim neighbours sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Order returned on the afternoon of 2 June, when Prime Minister Jamil Al Midfai imposed a curfew and had violators shot. Many sources give about 180 Jews killed, others nearly 200.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 7 June the restored government set up a committee of inquiry, and military courts sentenced a number of men to death, among them the minister Yunis al-Sabawi and army officers and policemen. Historians disagree on its significance. Some see it as the turning point for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s Jews, while others place that between 1948 and 1951, noting that many who left after the Farhud returned and that permanent emigration did not accelerate until 1950-1951. Orit Bashkin writes that the community strove for integration before and after the event. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> held the first International Farhud Day on 1 June 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Farhud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The period saw the Simele massacre of Assyrians in 1933, the Arab world's first military coup in 1936, and in 1941 Rashid Ali's pro-Axis coup, the Anglo-Iraqi War and the <b>Farhud</b> pogrom against Baghdad's Jews.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-iraq">Kingdom of Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
